From b55709bed07287fefc1c7714d9697b0bd3a25610 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ricardo Zuasti <rzuasti@pm.me>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 10:28:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] feat: retry requests for messages that fail to decrypt

Signal clients recover messages that fail to decrypt with retry
requests (DecryptionErrorMessage): the sender resends the message, and
resets the session if it was the cause.

- Decryption failures from a known sender return
  ServiceError::DecryptionFailed with what a retry request needs: the
  sender and device, the timestamp, the sealed sender content hint and
  group ID, and the DecryptionErrorMessage (with the ratchet key of
  1:1 messages). Duplicates, self-sends, unsupported versions and
  plaintext messages don't; nor do sealed sender envelopes whose sender
  certificate is invalid (SealedSenderDecryptionError, which loses its
  sender field).
- MessageSender::send_retry_request sends it as unencrypted
  PlaintextContent, as the official clients do, so it doesn't depend on
  the broken session; sealed when unidentified access is given, with
  ContentHint::Implicit and the group ID. It goes to all of the
  sender's devices, as the server requires.
- MessageSender::resend_content sends a Content again to one recipient
  with its original timestamp and no sync copy, to answer retry
  requests (and send null messages).

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
